# The Plasma splash screen, for every account on this image — plan/17.
#
# ONE FILE, AND IT IS WHY THERE IS NO PER-USER SETUP ANYWHERE IN THIS TREE. KConfig cascades
# $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS underneath ~/.config, so /etc/xdg/ksplashrc is the image's default for any
# user who has not chosen otherwise — the same layer /etc/xdg/baloofilerc and the installer
# medium's /etc/xdg/kscreenlockerrc use. There is no skel copy to keep in sync, nothing for
# Calamares' users module to write, and System Settings -> Appearance -> Splash Screen still
# writes a user's own choice to ~/.config/ksplashrc and wins over this.
#
# That covers the three populations in one go:
#
#   @LIVE_USER@ on the product image     - reads it from the read-only root
#   @LIVE_USER@ on the installer medium  - same file, same root
#   accounts Calamares creates           - the installed system IS the desktop profile's root
#                                          EROFS, written to disk byte for byte, so this file is
#                                          already there before the account exists
#
# THEME, NOT LookAndFeelPackage, and the difference matters on the installer medium. ksplashqml
# resolves its theme in this order (SplashApp::SplashApp, then SplashWindow::setGeometry):
#
#   1. the command-line argument            - plasma-ksplash.service passes none
#   2. ksplashrc [KSplash] Theme            - THIS, and it wins outright
#   3. kdeglobals [KDE] LookAndFeelPackage  - which the installer profile sets to
#                                             @DISTRO_ID@-installer for the task-manager layout
#   4. the default Plasma/LookAndFeel package
#
# So the live session gets our splash AND its own panel layout, out of two small packages,
# instead of one package that would have to carry both and then ship the installer's layout
# script into the product.
#
# The value is a Plasma/LookAndFeel package id: /usr/share/plasma/look-and-feel/@DISTRO_ID@,
# installed by stage 40 from config/plasma/lookandfeel. Stage 40 asserts the two spellings
# agree, because a typo here is not an error anywhere — ksplashqml falls back to Breeze and the
# machine boots to somebody else's logo.
#
# Engine is stated rather than left to its default because it is checked in three places before
# anything is drawn (startplasma's startKSplashViaSystemd(), plasma-session's startup.cpp, and
# ksplashqml itself), and in all three a value other than KSplashQML means no splash at all.
[KSplash]
Engine=KSplashQML
Theme=@DISTRO_ID@
